Chapter 6. Exceptions and File Handling

In this chapter, you will dive into exceptions, file objects, @{code}for@{body} loops, and the @{code}os@{body} and @{code}sys@{body} modules. If you've used exceptions in another programming language, you can skim the first section to get a sense of Python's syntax. Be sure to tune in again for file handling.

@{" Chapter contents " LINK "MAIN"}

@ENDNODE

@NODE S1 "6.1. Handling Exceptions"
@TITLE "6.1. Handling Exceptions"
@NEXT "S2"
@PREV "S0"
@TOC "MAIN"
@SMARTWRAP

@{b}6.1. Handling Exceptions @{ub}

Like many other programming languages, Python has exception handling via @{code}try...except@{body} blocks.

Python uses @{code}try...except@{body} to handle exceptions and @{code}raise@{body} to generate them. Java and C++ use @{code}try...catch@{body} to handle exceptions, and @{code}throw@{body} to generate them.

Exceptions are everywhere in Python. Virtually every module in the standard Python library uses them, and Python itself will raise them in a lot of different circumstances. You've already seen them repeatedly throughout this book.

* Accessing a non-existent dictionary key will raise a @{code}KeyError@{body} exception.

* Searching a list for a non-existent value will raise a @{code}ValueError@{body} exception.

* Calling a non-existent method will raise an @{code}AttributeError@{body} exception.

* Referencing a non-existent variable will raise a @{code}NameError@{body} exception.

* Mixing datatypes without coercion will raise a @{code}TypeError@{body} exception.

In each of these cases, you were simply playing around in the Python IDE: an error occurred, the exception was printed (depending on your IDE, perhaps in an intentionally jarring shade of red), and that was that. This is called an @{i}unhandled@{ui} exception. When the exception was raised, there was no code to explicitly notice it and deal with it, so it bubbled its way back to the default behavior built in to Python, which is to spit out some debugging information and give up. In the IDE, that's no big deal, but if that happened while your actual Python program was running, the entire program would come to a screeching halt.

An exception doesn't need result in a complete program crash, though. Exceptions, when raised, can be @{i}handled@{ui}. Sometimes an exception is really because you have a bug in your code (like accessing a variable that doesn't exist), but many times, an exception is something you can anticipate. If you're opening a file, it might not exist. If you're connecting to a database, it might be unavailable, or you might not have the correct security credentials to access it. If you know a line of code may raise an exception, you should handle the exception using a @{code}try...except@{body} block.

@{b}Example 6.1. Opening a Non-Existent File@{ub}

@{code}
>>> fsock = open("/notthere", "r")
Traceback (innermost last):
File "", line 1, in ?
IOError: [Errno 2] No such file or directory: '/notthere'
>>> try:
... fsock = open("/notthere")
... except IOError:
... print "The file does not exist, exiting gracefully"
... print "This line will always print"
The file does not exist, exiting gracefully
This line will always print
@{body}

Using the built-in @{code}open@{body} function, you can try to open a file for reading (more on @{code}open@{body} in the next section). But the file doesn't exist, so this raises the @{code}IOError@{body} exception. Since you haven't provided any explicit check for an @{code}IOError@{body} exception, Python just prints out some debugging information about what happened and then gives up.

You're trying to open the same non-existent file, but this time you're doing it within a @{code}try...except@{body} block.

When the @{code}open@{body} method raises an @{code}IOError@{body} exception, you're ready for it. The @{code}except IOError:@{body} line catches the exception and executes your own block of code, which in this case just prints a more pleasant error message.

Once an exception has been handled, processing continues normally on the first line after the @{code}try...except@{body} block. Note that this line will always print, whether or not an exception occurs. If you really did have a file called @{code}notthere@{body} in your root directory, the call to @{code}open@{body} would succeed, the @{code}except@{body} clause would be ignored, and this line would still be executed.

Exceptions may seem unfriendly (after all, if you don't catch the exception, your entire program will crash), but consider the alternative. Would you rather get back an unusable file object to a non-existent file? You'd need to check its validity somehow anyway, and if you forgot, somewhere down the line, your program would give you strange errors somewhere down the line that you would need to trace back to the source. I'm sure you've experienced this, and you know it's not fun. With exceptions, errors occur immediately, and you can handle them in a standard way at the source of the problem.

@{b}6.1.1. Using Exceptions For Other Purposes @{ub}

There are a lot of other uses for exceptions besides handling actual error conditions. A common use in the standard Python library is to try to import a module, and then check whether it worked. Importing a module that does not exist will raise an @{code}ImportError@{body} exception. You can use this to define multiple levels of functionality based on which modules are available at run-time, or to support multiple platforms (where platform-specific code is separated into different modules).

You can also define your own exceptions by creating a class that inherits from the built-in @{code}Exception@{body} class, and then raise your exceptions with the @{code}raise@{body} command. See the further reading section if you're interested in doing this.

The next example demonstrates how to use an exception to support platform-specific functionality. This code comes from the @{code}getpass@{body} module, a wrapper module for getting a password from the user. Getting a password is accomplished differently on UNIX, Windows, and Mac OS platforms, but this code encapsulates all of those differences.

@{b}Example 6.2. Supporting Platform-Specific Functionality@{ub}

@{code}

# Bind the name getpass to the appropriate function
try:
import termios, TERMIOS
except ImportError:
try:
import msvcrt
except ImportError:
try:
from EasyDialogs import AskPassword
except ImportError:
getpass = default_getpass
else:
getpass = AskPassword
else:
getpass = win_getpass
else:
getpass = unix_getpass
@{body}

@{code}termios@{body} is a UNIX-specific module that provides low-level control over the input terminal. If this module is not available (because it's not on your system, or your system doesn't support it), the import fails and Python raises an @{code}ImportError@{body}, which you catch.

OK, you didn't have @{code}termios@{body}, so let's try @{code}msvcrt@{body}, which is a Windows-specific module that provides an API to many useful functions in the Microsoft Visual C++ runtime services. If this import fails, Python will raise an @{code}ImportError@{body}, which you catch.

If the first two didn't work, you try to import a function from @{code}EasyDialogs@{body}, which is a Mac OS-specific module that provides functions to pop up dialog boxes of various types. Once again, if this import fails, Python will raise an @{code}ImportError@{body}, which you catch.

None of these platform-specific modules is available (which is possible, since Python has been ported to a lot of different platforms), so you need to fall back on a default password input function (which is defined elsewhere in the @{code}getpass@{body} module). Notice what you're doing here: assigning the function @{code}default_getpass@{body} to the variable @{code}getpass@{body}. If you read the official @{code}getpass@{body} documentation, it tells you that the @{code}getpass@{body} module defines a @{code}getpass@{body} function. It does this by binding @{code}getpass@{body} to the correct function for your platform. Then when you call the @{code}getpass@{body} function, you're really calling a platform-specific function that this code has set up for you. You don't need to know or care which platform your code is running on -- just call @{code}getpass@{body}, and it will always do the right thing.

A @{code}try...except@{body} block can have an @{code}else@{body} clause, like an @{code}if@{body} statement. If no exception is raised during the @{code}try@{body} block, the @{code}else@{body} clause is executed afterwards. In this case, that means that the @{code}from EasyDialogs import AskPassword@{body} import worked, so you should bind @{code}getpass@{body} to the @{code}AskPassword@{body} function. Each of the other @{code}try...except@{body} blocks has similar @{code}else@{body} clauses to bind @{code}getpass@{body} to the appropriate function when you find an @{code}import@{body} that works.

@{b}Further Reading on Exception Handling@{ub}

* @{i}Python Tutorial@{ui} discusses defining and raising your own exceptions, and handling multiple exceptions at once.

* @{i}Python Library Reference@{ui} summarizes all the built-in exceptions.

* @{i}Python Library Reference@{ui} documents the getpass module.

* @{i}Python Library Reference@{ui} documents the @{code}traceback@{body} module, which provides low-level access to exception attributes after an exception is raised.

* @{i}Python Reference Manual@{ui} discusses the inner workings of the @{code}try...except@{body} block.

@{" Chapter contents " LINK "MAIN"}

@ENDNODE

@NODE S2 "6.2. Working with File Objects"
@TITLE "6.2. Working with File Objects"
@NEXT "S3"
@PREV "S1"
@TOC "MAIN"
@SMARTWRAP

@{b}6.2. Working with File Objects @{ub}

Python has a built-in function, @{code}open@{body}, for opening a file on disk. @{code}open@{body} returns a file object, which has methods and attributes for getting information about and manipulating the opened file.

@{b}Example 6.3. Opening a File@{ub}

@{code}
>>> f = open("/music/_singles/kairo.mp3", "rb")
>>> f

>>> f.mode
'rb'
>>> f.name
'/music/_singles/kairo.mp3'
@{body}

The @{code}open@{body} method can take up to three parameters: a filename, a mode, and a buffering parameter. Only the first one, the filename, is required; the other two are optional. If not specified, the file is opened for reading in text mode. Here you are opening the file for reading in binary mode. (@{code}print open.__doc__@{body} displays a great explanation of all the possible modes.)

The @{code}open@{body} function returns an object (by now, this should not surprise you). A file object has several useful attributes.

The @{code}mode@{body} attribute of a file object tells you in which mode the file was opened.

The @{code}name@{body} attribute of a file object tells you the name of the file that the file object has open.

@{b}6.2.1. Reading Files @{ub}

After you open a file, the first thing you'll want to do is read from it, as shown in the next example.

@{b}Example 6.4. Reading a File@{ub}

@{code}

>>> f

>>> f.tell()
0
>>> f.seek(-128, 2)
>>> f.tell()
7542909
>>> tagData = f.read(128)
>>> tagData
'TAGKAIRO****THE BEST GOA ***DJ MARY-JANE***
Rave Mix 2000http://mp3.com/DJMARYJANE \\037'
>>> f.tell()
7543037
@{body}

A file object maintains state about the file it has open. The @{code}tell@{body} method of a file object tells you your current position in the open file. Since you haven't done anything with this file yet, the current position is @{code}0@{body}, which is the beginning of the file.

The @{code}seek@{body} method of a file object moves to another position in the open file. The second parameter specifies what the first one means; @{code}0@{body} means move to an absolute position (counting from the start of the file), @{code}1@{body} means move to a relative position (counting from the current position), and @{code}2@{body} means move to a position relative to the end of the file. Since the MP3 tags you're looking for are stored at the end of the file, you use @{code}2@{body} and tell the file object to move to a position @{code}128@{body} bytes from the end of the file.

The @{code}tell@{body} method confirms that the current file position has moved.

The @{code}read@{body} method reads a specified number of bytes from the open file and returns a string with the data that was read. The optional parameter specifies the maximum number of bytes to read. If no parameter is specified, @{code}read@{body} will read until the end of the file. (You could have simply said @{code}read()@{body} here, since you know exactly where you are in the file and you are, in fact, reading the last 128 bytes.) The read data is assigned to the @{code}tagData@{body} variable, and the current position is updated based on how many bytes were read.

The @{code}tell@{body} method confirms that the current position has moved. If you do the math, you'll see that after reading 128 bytes, the position has been incremented by 128.

@{b}6.2.2. Closing Files @{ub}

Open files consume system resources, and depending on the file mode, other programs may not be able to access them. It's important to close files as soon as you're finished with them.

@{b}Example 6.5. Closing a File@{ub}

@{code}

>>> f

>>> f.closed
False
>>> f.close()
>>> f

>>> f.closed
True
>>> f.seek(0)
Traceback (innermost last):
File "", line 1, in ?
ValueError: I/O operation on closed file
>>> f.tell()
Traceback (innermost last):
File "", line 1, in ?
ValueError: I/O operation on closed file
>>> f.read()
Traceback (innermost last):
File "", line 1, in ?
ValueError: I/O operation on closed file
>>> f.close()
@{body}

The @{code}closed@{body} attribute of a file object indicates whether the object has a file open or not. In this case, the file is still open (@{code}closed@{body} is @{code}False@{body}).

To close a file, call the @{code}close@{body} method of the file object. This frees the lock (if any) that you were holding on the file, flushes buffered writes (if any) that the system hadn't gotten around to actually writing yet, and releases the system resources.

The @{code}closed@{body} attribute confirms that the file is closed.

Just because a file is closed doesn't mean that the file object ceases to exist. The variable @{code}f@{body} will continue to exist until it goes out of scope or gets manually deleted. However, none of the methods that manipulate an open file will work once the file has been closed; they all raise an exception.

Calling @{code}close@{body} on a file object whose file is already closed does @{i}not@{ui} raise an exception; it fails silently.

@{b}6.2.3. Handling I/O Errors @{ub}

Now you've seen enough to understand the file handling code in the @{code}fileinfo.py@{body} sample code from teh previous chapter. This example shows how to safely open and read from a file and gracefully handle errors.

@{b}Example 6.6. File Objects in @{code}MP3FileInfo@{body}@{ub}

@{code}

try:
fsock = open(filename, "rb", 0)
try:
fsock.seek(-128, 2)
tagdata = fsock.read(128)
finally:
fsock.close()
.
.
.
except IOError:
pass
@{body}

Because opening and reading files is risky and may raise an exception, all of this code is wrapped in a @{code}try...except@{body} block. (Hey, isn't standardized indentation great? This is where you start to appreciate it.)

The @{code}open@{body} function may raise an @{code}IOError@{body}. (Maybe the file doesn't exist.)

The @{code}seek@{body} method may raise an @{code}IOError@{body}. (Maybe the file is smaller than 128 bytes.)

The @{code}read@{body} method may raise an @{code}IOError@{body}. (Maybe the disk has a bad sector, or it's on a network drive and the network just went down.)

This is new: a @{code}try...finally@{body} block. Once the file has been opened successfully by the @{code}open@{body} function, you want to make absolutely sure that you close it, even if an exception is raised by the @{code}seek@{body} or @{code}read@{body} methods. That's what a @{code}try...finally@{body} block is for: code in the @{code}finally@{body} block will @{i}always@{ui} be executed, even if something in the @{code}try@{body} block raises an exception. Think of it as code that gets executed on the way out, regardless of what happened before.

At last, you handle your @{code}IOError@{body} exception. This could be the @{code}IOError@{body} exception raised by the call to @{code}open@{body}, @{code}seek@{body}, or @{code}read@{body}. Here, you really don't care, because all you're going to do is ignore it silently and continue. (Remember, @{code}pass@{body} is a Python statement that does nothing.) That's perfectly legal; "handling" an exception can mean explicitly doing nothing. It still counts as handled, and processing will continue normally on the next line of code after the @{code}try...except@{body} block.

@{b}6.2.4. Writing to Files @{ub}

As you would expect, you can also write to files in much the same way that you read from them. There are two basic file modes:

* "Append" mode will add data to the end of the file.

* "write" mode will overwrite the file.

Either mode will create the file automatically if it doesn't already exist, so there's never a need for any sort of fiddly "if the log file doesn't exist yet, create a new empty file just so you can open it for the first time" logic. Just open it and start writing.

@{b}Example 6.7. Writing to Files@{ub}

@{code}

>>> logfile = open('test.log', 'w')
>>> logfile.write('test succeeded')
>>> logfile.close()
>>> print file('test.log').read()
test succeeded
>>> logfile = open('test.log', 'a')
>>> logfile.write('line 2')
>>> logfile.close()
>>> print file('test.log').read()
test succeededline 2

@{body}

You start boldly by creating either the new file @{code}test.log@{body} or overwrites the existing file, and opening the file for writing. (The second parameter @{code}"w"@{body} means open the file for writing.) Yes, that's all as dangerous as it sounds. I hope you didn't care about the previous contents of that file, because it's gone now.

You can add data to the newly opened file with the @{code}write@{body} method of the file object returned by @{code}open@{body}.

@{code}file@{body} is a synonym for @{code}open@{body}. This one-liner opens the file, reads its contents, and prints them.

You happen to know that @{code}test.log@{body} exists (since you just finished writing to it), so you can open it and append to it. (The @{code}"a"@{body} parameter means open the file for appending.) Actually you could do this even if the file didn't exist, because opening the file for appending will create the file if necessary. But appending will @{i}never@{ui} harm the existing contents of the file.

As you can see, both the original line you wrote and the second line you appended are now in @{code}test.log@{body}. Also note that carriage returns are not included. Since you didn't write them explicitly to the file either time, the file doesn't include them. You can write a carriage return with the @{code}"\\n"@{body} character. Since you didn't do this, everything you wrote to the file ended up smooshed together on the same line.

@{b}Further Reading on File Handling@{ub}

* @{i}Python Tutorial@{ui} discusses reading and writing files, including how to read a file one line at a time into a list.

* eff-bot discusses efficiency and performance of various ways of reading a file.

* Python Knowledge Base answers common questions about files.

* @{i}Python Library Reference@{ui} summarizes all the file object methods.

@{" Chapter contents " LINK "MAIN"}

@ENDNODE

@NODE S3 "6.3. Iterating with for Loops"
@TITLE "6.3. Iterating with for Loops"
@NEXT "S4"
@PREV "S2"
@TOC "MAIN"
@SMARTWRAP

@{b}6.3. Iterating with @{code}for@{body} Loops @{ub}

Like most other languages, Python has @{code}for@{body} loops. The only reason you haven't seen them until now is that Python is good at so many other things that you don't need them as often.

Most other languages don't have a powerful list datatype like Python, so you end up doing a lot of manual work, specifying a start, end, and step to define a range of integers or characters or other iteratable entities. But in Python, a @{code}for@{body} loop simply iterates over a list, the same way list comprehensions work.

@{b}Example 6.8. Introducing the @{code}for@{body} Loop @{ub}

@{code}
>>> li = ['a', 'b', 'e']
>>> for s in li:
... print s
a
b
e
>>> print "\\n".join(li)
a
b
e
@{body}

The syntax for a @{code}for@{body} loop is similar to list comprehensions. @{code}li@{body} is a list, and @{code}s@{body} will take the value of each element in turn, starting from the first element.

Like an @{code}if@{body} statement or any other indented block, a @{code}for@{body} loop can have any number of lines of code in it.

This is the reason you haven't seen the @{code}for@{body} loop yet: you haven't needed it yet. It's amazing how often you use @{code}for@{body} loops in other languages when all you really want is a @{code}join@{body} or a list comprehension.

Doing a "normal" (by Visual Basic standards) counter @{code}for@{body} loop is also simple.

@{b}Example 6.9. Simple Counters@{ub}

@{code}

>>> for i in range(5):
... print i
0
1
2
3
4
>>> li = ['a', 'b', 'c', 'd', 'e']
>>> for i in range(len(li)):
... print li[i]
a
b
c
d
e

@{body}

As you saw in Example 3.20, "Assigning Consecutive Values", @{code}range@{body} produces a list of integers, which you then loop through. I know it looks a bit odd, but it is occasionally (and I stress @{i}occasionally@{ui}) useful to have a counter loop.

Don't ever do this. This is Visual Basic-style thinking. Break out of it. Just iterate through the list, as shown in the previous example.

@{code}for@{body} loops are not just for simple counters. They can iterate through all kinds of things. Here is an example of using a @{code}for@{body} loop to iterate through a dictionary.

@{b}Example 6.10. Iterating Through a Dictionary@{ub}

@{code}

>>> import os
>>> for k, v in os.environ.items():
... print "%s=%s" % (k, v)
USERPROFILE=C:\\Documents and Settings\\mpilgrim
OS=Windows_NT
COMPUTERNAME=MPILGRIM
USERNAME=mpilgrim

[...snip...]
>>> print "\\n".join(["%s=%s" % (k, v)
... for k, v in os.environ.items()])
USERPROFILE=C:\\Documents and Settings\\mpilgrim
OS=Windows_NT
COMPUTERNAME=MPILGRIM
USERNAME=mpilgrim

[...snip...]
@{body}

@{code}os.environ@{body} is a dictionary of the environment variables defined on your system. In Windows, these are your user and system variables accessible from MS-DOS. In UNIX, they are the variables exported in your shell's startup scripts. In Mac OS, there is no concept of environment variables, so this dictionary is empty.

@{code}os.environ.items()@{body} returns a list of tuples: @{code}[(@{i}key1@{ui}, @{i}value1@{ui}), (@{i}key2@{ui}, @{i}value2@{ui}), ...]@{body}. The @{code}for@{body} loop iterates through this list. The first round, it assigns @{code}@{i}key1@{ui}@{body} to @{code}k@{body} and @{code}@{i}value1@{ui}@{body} to @{code}v@{body}, so @{code}k@{body} = @{code}USERPROFILE@{body} and @{code}v@{body} = @{code}C:\\Documents and Settings\\mpilgrim@{body}. In the second round, @{code}k@{body} gets the second key, @{code}OS@{body}, and @{code}v@{body} gets the corresponding value, @{code}Windows_NT@{body}.

With multi-variable assignment and list comprehensions, you can replace the entire @{code}for@{body} loop with a single statement. Whether you actually do this in real code is a matter of personal coding style. I like it because it makes it clear that what I'm doing is mapping a dictionary into a list, then joining the list into a single string. Other programmers prefer to write this out as a @{code}for@{body} loop. The output is the same in either case, although this version is slightly faster, because there is only one @{code}print@{body} statement instead of many.

Now we can look at the @{code}for@{body} loop in @{code}MP3FileInfo@{body}, from the sample @{code}fileinfo.py@{body} program introduced in Chapter 5.

@{b}Example 6.11. @{code}for@{body} Loop in @{code}MP3FileInfo@{body}@{ub}

@{code}

tagDataMap = {"title" : ( 3, 33, stripnulls),
"artist" : ( 33, 63, stripnulls),
"album" : ( 63, 93, stripnulls),
"year" : ( 93, 97, stripnulls),
"comment" : ( 97, 126, stripnulls),
"genre" : (127, 128, ord)}
.
.
.
if tagdata[:3] == "TAG":
for tag, (start, end, parseFunc) in self.tagDataMap.items():
self[tag] = parseFunc(tagdata[start:end])
@{body}

@{code}tagDataMap@{body} is a class attribute that defines the tags you're looking for in an MP3 file. Tags are stored in fixed-length fields. Once you read the last 128 bytes of the file, bytes 3 through 32 of those are always the song title, 33 through 62 are always the artist name, 63 through 92 are the album name, and so forth. Note that @{code}tagDataMap@{body} is a dictionary of tuples, and each tuple contains two integers and a function reference.

This looks complicated, but it's not. The structure of the @{code}for@{body} variables matches the structure of the elements of the list returned by @{code}items@{body}. Remember that @{code}items@{body} returns a list of tuples of the form @{code}(@{i}key@{ui}, @{i}value@{ui})@{body}. The first element of that list is @{code}("title", (3, 33, ))@{body}, so the first time around the loop, @{code}tag@{body} gets @{code}"title"@{body}, @{code}start@{body} gets @{code}3@{body}, @{code}end@{body} gets @{code}33@{body}, and @{code}parseFunc@{body} gets the function @{code}stripnulls@{body}.

Now that you've extracted all the parameters for a single MP3 tag, saving the tag data is easy. You slice @{code}tagdata@{body} from @{code}start@{body} to @{code}end@{body} to get the actual data for this tag, call @{code}parseFunc@{body} to post-process the data, and assign this as the value for the key @{code}tag@{body} in the pseudo-dictionary @{code}self@{body}. After iterating through all the elements in @{code}tagDataMap@{body}, @{code}self@{body} has the values for all the tags, and you know what that looks like.

@{" Chapter contents " LINK "MAIN"}

@ENDNODE

