@SMARTWRAP

@{b}6.4. Using @{code}@{code}sys@{body}.modules@{body}@{ub}

Modules, like everything else in Python, are objects. Once imported, you can always get a reference to a module through the global dictionary @{code}@{code}sys@{body}.modules@{body}.

@{b}Example 6.12. Introducing @{code}@{code}sys@{body}.modules@{body}@{ub}

@{code}
>>> import sys
>>> print '\\n'.join(sys.modules.keys())
win32api
os.path
os
exceptions
__main__
ntpath
nt
sys
__builtin__
site
signal
UserDict
stat
@{body}

The @{code}sys@{body} module contains system-level information, such as the version of Python you're running (@{code}@{code}sys@{body}.version@{body} or @{code}@{code}sys@{body}.version_info@{body}), and system-level options such as the maximum allowed recursion depth (@{code}@{code}sys@{body}.getrecursionlimit()@{body} and @{code}@{code}sys@{body}.setrecursionlimit()@{body}).

@{code}@{code}sys@{body}.modules@{body} is a dictionary containing all the modules that have ever been imported since Python was started; the key is the module name, the value is the module object. Note that this is more than just the modules @{i}your@{ui} program has imported. Python preloads some modules on startup, and if you're using a Python IDE, @{code}@{code}sys@{body}.modules@{body} contains all the modules imported by all the programs you've run within the IDE.

This example demonstrates how to use @{code}@{code}sys@{body}.modules@{body}.

@{b}Example 6.13. Using @{code}@{code}sys@{body}.modules@{body}@{ub}

@{code}
>>> import fileinfo
>>> print '\\n'.join(sys.modules.keys())
win32api
os.path
os
fileinfo
exceptions
__main__
ntpath
nt
sys
__builtin__
site
signal
UserDict
stat
>>> fileinfo

>>> sys.modules["fileinfo"]

@{body}

As new modules are imported, they are added to @{code}@{code}sys@{body}.modules@{body}. This explains why importing the same module twice is very fast: Python has already loaded and cached the module in @{code}@{code}sys@{body}.modules@{body}, so importing the second time is simply a dictionary lookup.

Given the name (as a string) of any previously-imported module, you can get a reference to the module itself through the @{code}@{code}sys@{body}.modules@{body} dictionary.

The next example shows how to use the @{code}__module__@{body} class attribute with the @{code}@{code}sys@{body}.modules@{body} dictionary to get a reference to the module in which a class is defined.

@{b}Example 6.14. The @{code}__module__@{body} Class Attribute @{ub}

@{code}
>>> from fileinfo import MP3FileInfo
>>> MP3FileInfo.__module__
'fileinfo'
>>> sys.modules[MP3FileInfo.__module__]

@{body}

Every Python class has a built-in class attribute @{code}__module__@{body}, which is the name of the module in which the class is defined.

Combining this with the @{code}@{code}sys@{body}.modules@{body} dictionary, you can get a reference to the module in which a class is defined.

Now you're ready to see how @{code}@{code}sys@{body}.modules@{body} is used in @{code}fileinfo.py@{body}, the sample program introduced in Chapter 5. This example shows that portion of the code.

@{b}Example 6.15. @{code}@{code}sys@{body}.modules@{body} in @{code}fileinfo.py@{body}@{ub}

@{code}

def getFileInfoClass(filename, module=sys.modules[FileInfo.__module__]):
"get file info class from filename extension"
subclass = "%sFileInfo" % os.path.splitext(filename)[1].upper()[1:]
return hasattr(module, subclass) and getattr(module, subclass) or FileInfo
@{body}

This is a function with two arguments; @{code}filename@{body} is required, but @{code}module@{body} is optional and defaults to the module that contains the @{code}FileInfo@{body} class. This looks inefficient, because you might expect Python to evaluate the @{code}@{code}sys@{body}.modules@{body} expression every time the function is called. In fact, Python evaluates default expressions only once, the first time the module is imported. As you'll see later, you never call this function with a @{code}module@{body} argument, so @{code}module@{body} serves as a function-level constant.

You'll plow through this line later, after you dive into the @{code}os@{body} module. For now, take it on faith that @{code}subclass@{body} ends up as the name of a class, like @{code}MP3FileInfo@{body}.

You already know about @{code}getattr@{body}, which gets a reference to an object by name. @{code}hasattr@{body} is a complementary function that checks whether an object has a particular attribute; in this case, whether a module has a particular class (although it works for any object and any attribute, just like @{code}getattr@{body}). In English, this line of code says, "If this module has the class named by @{code}subclass@{body} then return it, otherwise return the base class @{code}FileInfo@{body}."

@{b}Further Reading on Modules@{ub}

* @{i}Python Tutorial@{ui} discusses exactly when and how default arguments are evaluated.

* @{i}Python Library Reference@{ui} documents the @{code}sys@{body} module.

@{" Chapter contents " LINK "MAIN"}

@ENDNODE

@NODE S5 "6.5. Working with Directories"
@TITLE "6.5. Working with Directories"
@NEXT "S6"
@PREV "S4"
@TOC "MAIN"
@SMARTWRAP

@{b}6.5. Working with Directories @{ub}

The @{code}os.path@{body} module has several functions for manipulating files and directories. Here, we're looking at handling pathnames and listing the contents of a directory.

@{b}Example 6.16. Constructing Pathnames@{ub}

@{code}

>>> import os
>>> os.path.join("c:\\\\music\\\\ap\\\\", "mahadeva.mp3")
'c:\\\\music\\\\ap\\\\mahadeva.mp3'
>>> os.path.join("c:\\\\music\\\\ap", "mahadeva.mp3")
'c:\\\\music\\\\ap\\\\mahadeva.mp3'
>>> os.path.expanduser("~")
'c:\\\\Documents and Settings\\\\mpilgrim\\\\My Documents'
>>> os.path.join(os.path.expanduser("~"), "Python")
'c:\\\\Documents and Settings\\\\mpilgrim\\\\My Documents\\\\Python'
@{body}

@{code}os.path@{body} is a reference to a module -- which module depends on your platform. Just as @{code}getpass@{body} encapsulates differences between platforms by setting @{code}getpass@{body} to a platform-specific function, @{code}os@{body} encapsulates differences between platforms by setting @{code}path@{body} to a platform-specific module.

The @{code}join@{body} function of @{code}os.path@{body} constructs a pathname out of one or more partial pathnames. In this case, it simply concatenates strings. (Note that dealing with pathnames on Windows is annoying because the backslash character must be escaped.)

In this slightly less trivial case, @{code}join@{body} will add an extra backslash to the pathname before joining it to the filename. I was overjoyed when I discovered this, since @{code}addSlashIfNecessary@{body} is one of the stupid little functions I always need to write when building up my toolbox in a new language. @{i}Do not@{ui} write this stupid little function in Python; smart people have already taken care of it for you.

@{code}expanduser@{body} will expand a pathname that uses @{code}~@{body} to represent the current user's home directory. This works on any platform where users have a home directory, like Windows, UNIX, and Mac OS X; it has no effect on Mac OS.

Combining these techniques, you can easily construct pathnames for directories and files under the user's home directory.

@{b}Example 6.17. Splitting Pathnames@{ub}

@{code}
>>> os.path.split("c:\\\\music\\\\ap\\\\mahadeva.mp3")
('c:\\\\music\\\\ap', 'mahadeva.mp3')
>>> (filepath, filename) = os.path.split("c:\\\\music\\\\ap\\\\mahadeva.mp3")
>>> filepath
'c:\\\\music\\\\ap'
>>> filename
'mahadeva.mp3'
>>> (shortname, extension) = os.path.splitext(filename)
>>> shortname
'mahadeva'
>>> extension
'.mp3'
@{body}

The @{code}split@{body} function splits a full pathname and returns a tuple containing the path and filename. Remember when I said you could use multi-variable assignment to return multiple values from a function? Well, @{code}split@{body} is such a function.

You assign the return value of the @{code}split@{body} function into a tuple of two variables. Each variable receives the value of the corresponding element of the returned tuple.

The first variable, @{code}filepath@{body}, receives the value of the first element of the tuple returned from @{code}split@{body}, the file path.

The second variable, @{code}filename@{body}, receives the value of the second element of the tuple returned from @{code}split@{body}, the filename.

@{code}os.path@{body} also contains a function @{code}splitext@{body}, which splits a filename and returns a tuple containing the filename and the file extension. You use the same technique to assign each of them to separate variables.

@{b}Example 6.18. Listing Directories@{ub}

@{code}
>>> os.listdir("c:\\\\music\\\\_singles\\\\")
['a_time_long_forgotten_con.mp3', 'hellraiser.mp3',
'kairo.mp3', 'long_way_home1.mp3', 'sidewinder.mp3',
'spinning.mp3']
>>> dirname = "c:\\\\"
>>> os.listdir(dirname)
['AUTOEXEC.BAT', 'boot.ini', 'CONFIG.SYS', 'cygwin',
'docbook', 'Documents and Settings', 'Incoming', 'Inetpub', 'IO.SYS',
'MSDOS.SYS', 'Music', 'NTDETECT.COM', 'ntldr', 'pagefile.sys',
'Program Files', 'Python20', 'RECYCLER',
'System Volume Information', 'TEMP', 'WINNT']
>>> [f for f in os.listdir(dirname)
... if os.path.isfile(os.path.join(dirname, f))]
['AUTOEXEC.BAT', 'boot.ini', 'CONFIG.SYS', 'IO.SYS', 'MSDOS.SYS',
'NTDETECT.COM', 'ntldr', 'pagefile.sys']
>>> [f for f in os.listdir(dirname)
... if os.path.isdir(os.path.join(dirname, f))]
['cygwin', 'docbook', 'Documents and Settings', 'Incoming',
'Inetpub', 'Music', 'Program Files', 'Python20', 'RECYCLER',
'System Volume Information', 'TEMP', 'WINNT']
@{body}

The @{code}listdir@{body} function takes a pathname and returns a list of the contents of the directory.

@{code}listdir@{body} returns both files and folders, with no indication of which is which.

You can use list filtering and the @{code}isfile@{body} function of the @{code}os.path@{body} module to separate the files from the folders. @{code}isfile@{body} takes a pathname and returns 1 if the path represents a file, and 0 otherwise. Here you're using @{code}@{code}os.path@{body}.@{code}join@{body}@{body} to ensure a full pathname, but @{code}isfile@{body} also works with a partial path, relative to the current working directory. You can use @{code}os.getcwd()@{body} to get the current working directory.

@{code}os.path@{body} also has a @{code}isdir@{body} function which returns 1 if the path represents a directory, and 0 otherwise. You can use this to get a list of the subdirectories within a directory.

@{b}Example 6.19. Listing Directories in @{code}fileinfo.py@{body}@{ub}

@{code}

def listDirectory(directory, fileExtList):
"get list of file info objects for files of particular extensions"
fileList = [os.path.normcase(f)
for f in os.listdir(directory)]
fileList = [os.path.join(directory, f)
for f in fileList
if os.path.splitext(f)[1] in fileExtList]
@{body}

@{code}os.listdir(directory)@{body} returns a list of all the files and folders in @{code}directory@{body}.

Iterating through the list with @{code}f@{body}, you use @{code}os.path.normcase(f)@{body} to normalize the case according to operating system defaults. @{code}normcase@{body} is a useful little function that compensates for case-insensitive operating systems that think that @{code}mahadeva.mp3@{body} and @{code}mahadeva.MP3@{body} are the same file. For instance, on Windows and Mac OS, @{code}normcase@{body} will convert the entire filename to lowercase; on UNIX-compatible systems, it will return the filename unchanged.

Iterating through the normalized list with @{code}f@{body} again, you use @{code}os.path.splitext(f)@{body} to split each filename into name and extension.

For each file, you see if the extension is in the list of file extensions you care about (@{code}fileExtList@{body}, which was passed to the @{code}listDirectory@{body} function).

For each file you care about, you use @{code}os.path.join(directory, f)@{body} to construct the full pathname of the file, and return a list of the full pathnames.

Whenever possible, you should use the functions in @{code}os@{body} and @{code}os.path@{body} for file, directory, and path manipulations. These modules are wrappers for platform-specific modules, so functions like @{code}os.path.split@{body} work on UNIX, Windows, Mac OS, and any other platform supported by Python.

There is one other way to get the contents of a directory. It's very powerful, and it uses the sort of wildcards that you may already be familiar with from working on the command line.

@{b}Example 6.20. Listing Directories with @{code}glob@{body}@{ub}

@{code}

>>> os.listdir("c:\\\\music\\\\_singles\\\\")
['a_time_long_forgotten_con.mp3', 'hellraiser.mp3',
'kairo.mp3', 'long_way_home1.mp3', 'sidewinder.mp3',
'spinning.mp3']
>>> import glob
>>> glob.glob('c:\\\\music\\\\_singles\\\\*.mp3')
['c:\\\\music\\\\_singles\\\\a_time_long_forgotten_con.mp3',
'c:\\\\music\\\\_singles\\\\hellraiser.mp3',
'c:\\\\music\\\\_singles\\\\kairo.mp3',
'c:\\\\music\\\\_singles\\\\long_way_home1.mp3',
'c:\\\\music\\\\_singles\\\\sidewinder.mp3',
'c:\\\\music\\\\_singles\\\\spinning.mp3']
>>> glob.glob('c:\\\\music\\\\_singles\\\\s*.mp3')
['c:\\\\music\\\\_singles\\\\sidewinder.mp3',
'c:\\\\music\\\\_singles\\\\spinning.mp3']
>>> glob.glob('c:\\\\music\\\\*\\\\*.mp3')

@{body}

As you saw earlier, @{code}os.listdir@{body} simply takes a directory path and lists all files and directories in that directory.

The @{code}glob@{body} module, on the other hand, takes a wildcard and returns the full path of all files and directories matching the wildcard. Here the wildcard is a directory path plus "*.mp3", which will match all @{code}.mp3@{body} files. Note that each element of the returned list already includes the full path of the file.

If you want to find all the files in a specific directory that start with "s" and end with ".mp3", you can do that too.

Now consider this scenario: you have a @{code}music@{body} directory, with several subdirectories within it, with @{code}.mp3@{body} files within each subdirectory. You can get a list of all of those with a single call to @{code}glob@{body}, by using two wildcards at once. One wildcard is the @{code}"*.mp3"@{body} (to match @{code}.mp3@{body} files), and one wildcard is @{i}within the directory path itself@{ui}, to match any subdirectory within @{code}c:\\music@{body}. That's a crazy amount of power packed into one deceptively simple-looking function!

@{b}Further Reading on the @{code}os@{body} Module @{ub}

* Python Knowledge Base answers questions about the @{code}os@{body} module.

* @{i}Python Library Reference@{ui} documents the @{code}os@{body} module and the @{code}os.path@{body} module.

@{" Chapter contents " LINK "MAIN"}

@ENDNODE

@NODE S6 "6.6. Putting It All Together"
@TITLE "6.6. Putting It All Together"
@NEXT "S7"
@PREV "S5"
@TOC "MAIN"
@SMARTWRAP

@{b}6.6. Putting It All Together @{ub}

Once again, all the dominoes are in place. You've seen how each line of code works. Now let's step back and see how it all fits together.

@{b}Example 6.21. @{code}listDirectory@{body}@{ub}

@{code}

def listDirectory(directory, fileExtList):
"get list of file info objects for files of particular extensions"
fileList = [os.path.normcase(f)
for f in os.listdir(directory)]
fileList = [os.path.join(directory, f)
for f in fileList
if os.path.splitext(f)[1] in fileExtList]
def getFileInfoClass(filename, module=sys.modules[FileInfo.__module__]):
"get file info class from filename extension"
subclass = "%sFileInfo" % os.path.splitext(filename)[1].upper()[1:]
return hasattr(module, subclass) and getattr(module, subclass) or FileInfo
return [getFileInfoClass(f)(f) for f in fileList]
@{body}

@{code}listDirectory@{body} is the main attraction of this entire module. It takes a directory (like @{code}c:\\music\\_singles\\@{body} in my case) and a list of interesting file extensions (like @{code}['.mp3']@{body}), and it returns a list of class instances that act like dictionaries that contain metadata about each interesting file in that directory. And it does it in just a few straightforward lines of code.

As you saw in the previous section, this line of code gets a list of the full pathnames of all the files in @{code}directory@{body} that have an interesting file extension (as specified by @{code}fileExtList@{body}).

Old-school Pascal programmers may be familiar with them, but most people give me a blank stare when I tell them that Python supports @{i}nested functions@{ui} -- literally, a function within a function. The nested function @{code}getFileInfoClass@{body} can be called only from the function in which it is defined, @{code}listDirectory@{body}. As with any other function, you don't need an interface declaration or anything fancy; just define the function and code it.

Now that you've seen the @{code}os@{body} module, this line should make more sense. It gets the extension of the file (@{code}os.path.splitext(filename)[1]@{body}), forces it to uppercase (@{code}.upper()@{body}), slices off the dot (@{code}[1:]@{body}), and constructs a class name out of it with string formatting. So @{code}c:\\music\\ap\\mahadeva.mp3@{body} becomes @{code}.mp3@{body} becomes @{code}.MP3@{body} becomes @{code}MP3@{body} becomes @{code}MP3FileInfo@{body}.

Having constructed the name of the handler class that would handle this file, you check to see if that handler class actually exists in this module. If it does, you return the class, otherwise you return the base class @{code}FileInfo@{body}. This is a very important point: @{i}this function returns a class@{ui}. Not an instance of a class, but the class itself.

For each file in the "interesting files" list (@{code}fileList@{body}), you call @{code}getFileInfoClass@{body} with the filename (@{code}f@{body}). Calling @{code}getFileInfoClass(f)@{body} returns a class; you don't know exactly which class, but you don't care. You then create an instance of this class (whatever it is) and pass the filename (@{code}f@{body} again), to the @{code}__init__@{body} method. As you saw earlier in this chapter, the @{code}__init__@{body} method of @{code}FileInfo@{body} sets @{code}self["name"]@{body}, which triggers @{code}__setitem__@{body}, which is overridden in the descendant (@{code}MP3FileInfo@{body}) to parse the file appropriately to pull out the file's metadata. You do all that for each interesting file and return a list of the resulting instances.

Note that @{code}listDirectory@{body} is completely generic. It doesn't know ahead of time which types of files it will be getting, or which classes are defined that could potentially handle those files. It inspects the directory for the files to process, and then introspects its own module to see what special handler classes (like @{code}MP3FileInfo@{body}) are defined. You can extend this program to handle other types of files simply by defining an appropriately-named class: @{code}HTMLFileInfo@{body} for HTML files, @{code}DOCFileInfo@{body} for Word @{code}.doc@{body} files, and so forth. @{code}listDirectory@{body} will handle them all, without modification, by handing off the real work to the appropriate classes and collating the results.

@{" Chapter contents " LINK "MAIN"}

@ENDNODE

@NODE S7 "6.7. Summary"
@TITLE "6.7. Summary"
@NEXT "MAIN"
@PREV "S6"
@TOC "MAIN"
@SMARTWRAP

@{b}6.7. Summary @{ub}

The @{code}fileinfo.py@{body} program introduced in Chapter 5 should now make perfect sense.

@{code}

"""Framework for getting filetype-specific metadata.

Instantiate appropriate class with filename. Returned object acts like a
dictionary, with key-value pairs for each piece of metadata.
import fileinfo
info = fileinfo.MP3FileInfo("/music/ap/mahadeva.mp3")
print "\\\\n".join(["%s=%s" % (k, v) for k, v in info.items()])

Or use listDirectory function to get info on all files in a directory.
for info in fileinfo.listDirectory("/music/ap/", [".mp3"]):
...

Framework can be extended by adding classes for particular file types, e.g.
HTMLFileInfo, MPGFileInfo, DOCFileInfo. Each class is completely responsible for
parsing its files appropriately; see MP3FileInfo for example.
"""
import os
import sys
from UserDict import UserDict

def stripnulls(data):
"strip whitespace and nulls"
return data.replace("\\00", "").strip()

class FileInfo(UserDict):
"store file metadata"
def __init__(self, filename=None):
UserDict.__init__(self)
self["name"] = filename

class MP3FileInfo(FileInfo):
"store ID3v1.0 MP3 tags"
tagDataMap = {"title" : ( 3, 33, stripnulls),
"artist" : ( 33, 63, stripnulls),
"album" : ( 63, 93, stripnulls),
"year" : ( 93, 97, stripnulls),
"comment" : ( 97, 126, stripnulls),
"genre" : (127, 128, ord)}

def __parse(self, filename):
"parse ID3v1.0 tags from MP3 file"
self.clear()
try:
fsock = open(filename, "rb", 0)
try:
fsock.seek(-128, 2)
tagdata = fsock.read(128)
finally:
fsock.close()
if tagdata[:3] == "TAG":
for tag, (start, end, parseFunc) in self.tagDataMap.items():
self[tag] = parseFunc(tagdata[start:end])
except IOError:
pass

def __setitem__(self, key, item):
if key == "name" and item:
self.__parse(item)
FileInfo.__setitem__(self, key, item)

def listDirectory(directory, fileExtList):
"get list of file info objects for files of particular extensions"
fileList = [os.path.normcase(f)
for f in os.listdir(directory)]
fileList = [os.path.join(directory, f)
for f in fileList
if os.path.splitext(f)[1] in fileExtList]
def getFileInfoClass(filename, module=sys.modules[FileInfo.__module__]):
"get file info class from filename extension"
subclass = "%sFileInfo" % os.path.splitext(filename)[1].upper()[1:]
return hasattr(module, subclass) and getattr(module, subclass) or FileInfo
return [getFileInfoClass(f)(f) for f in fileList]

if __name__ == "__main__":
for info in listDirectory("/music/_singles/", [".mp3"]):
print "\\n".join(["%s=%s" % (k, v) for k, v in info.items()])
print
@{body}

Before diving into the next chapter, make sure you're comfortable doing the following things:

* Catching exceptions with @{code}try...except@{body}

* Protecting external resources with @{code}try...finally@{body}

* Reading from files

* Assigning multiple values at once in a @{code}for@{body} loop

* Using the @{code}os@{body} module for all your cross-platform file manipulation needs

* Dynamically instantiating classes of unknown type by treating classes as objects and passing them around

@{b}<a

@{" Chapter contents " LINK "MAIN"}

@ENDNODE
